      "writtenOn": "2025-07-31T13:32:44Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Adding Kai: I now understand the semantic in the JS API but it\u0027s weird to me that we allow passing in the chained descriptor in C even when the extension is not enabled. In JS, WebIDL ignores unknown dictionary members, but in C we produce a validation error on unknown chains.\n\nThis means that there is a difference when passing a default swizzle between a webgpu.h implementation that doesn\u0027t know the extension (validation error) and on that knows the implementation, even if the feature isn\u0027t enabled (no validation error).",
      "parentUuid": "c573e78f_69d69e50",
      "revId": "90f42403662047f40475d6781ca73a5d21a853e3",
      "serverId": "dd02978d-1a8e-36d7-bcc0-a5723e5c0abd"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "33ab8e91_d6e0be2a",
        "filename": "src/dawn/native/Texture.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 486,
      "author": {
        "id": 1000014
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-07-31T20:32:58Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I think other similar cases in the C API produce validation errors when passing an extension struct even if it has the default values. I think that is fine to do here, it just means that Blink will have to avoid passing the extension when the feature is not enabled.\n\nIn general I\u0027m undecided which option is better for C. It\u0027s already true that applications should be tested to make sure it works correctly on all of its target implementations (especially if they use extensions) so it probably wouldn\u0027t be that bad if Dawn accepts the extension struct even if the feature is disabled. A third option if we wanted to make the C API more like the JS API, we could have implementations ignore chained structs with unknown sTypes instead of erroring.",
      "parentUuid": "08b91d38_e70ba83b",
      "revId": "90f42403662047f40475d6781ca73a5d21a853e3",
      "serverId": "dd02978d-1a8e-36d7-bcc0-a5723e5c0abd"
    }
